## Proof

Throughout, let $X$ be a topological space, $a \in X$, and $\mathcal{N}_a$ the set of neighborhoods of $a$. Recall
$$\omega_f(a)=\inf_{V\in\mathcal N_a}\Big\{\sup_{x,y\in V}|f(x)-f(y)|\Big\}.$$

### a. $f$ is continuous at $a$ $\iff$ $\omega_f(a)=0$.

**($\Rightarrow$) Suppose $f$ is continuous at $a$.** Let $\varepsilon>0$. By continuity there exists $V\in\mathcal N_a$ such that $|f(x)-f(a)|<\varepsilon/2$ for every $x\in V$. Hence for all $x,y\in V$,
$$|f(x)-f(y)|\le |f(x)-f(a)|+|f(a)-f(y)|<\varepsilon,$$
so $\sup_{x,y\in V}|f(x)-f(y)|\le \varepsilon$. Therefore $\omega_f(a)\le\varepsilon$. Since $\varepsilon>0$ was arbitrary, $\omega_f(a)=0$.

**($\Leftarrow$) Suppose $\omega_f(a)=0$.** Let $\varepsilon>0$. Since $\omega_f(a)=0<\varepsilon$, by definition of the infimum there exists $V\in\mathcal N_a$ with
$$\sup_{x,y\in V}|f(x)-f(y)|<\varepsilon.$$
In particular, taking $y=a$ (note $a\in V$ since $V$ is a neighborhood of $a$), we get $|f(x)-f(a)|<\varepsilon$ for every $x\in V$. Thus $f$ is continuous at $a$. $\quad\blacksquare$

---

### b. Let $K\subset\mathbb R$ be an open interval and $f:K\to\mathbb R$ monotone. Then for every $a\in K$,
$$\omega_f(a)=\Big|\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)-\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)\Big|.$$

Assume $f$ is **increasing** (the decreasing case is identical with signs reversed, or follows by applying the result to $-f$). Since $K$ is an open interval and $a\in K$, the point $a$ is interior to $K$, so there exist points of $K$ on both sides of $a$. A monotone function on an interval possesses finite one-sided limits at every interior point; set
$$L^-:=\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)=\sup_{x<a}f(x),\qquad L^+:=\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)=\inf_{x>a}f(x).$$
Both are finite because $L^-\le f(a)\le L^+$ and $f(a)\in\mathbb R$, and $L^-\ge f(x_0)$, $L^+\le f(y_0)$ for some $x_0<a<y_0$ in $K$. In particular $L^+\ge L^-$, so $|L^+-L^-|=L^+-L^-$.

For an increasing function and any set $S\subset K$,
$$\sup_{x,y\in S}|f(x)-f(y)|=\sup_S f-\inf_S f.$$

**Lower bound.** Let $V\in\mathcal N_a$ (neighborhood of $a$ in $K$). Since $K$ carries the subspace topology and $a$ is interior, $V$ contains an open interval $(a-\delta,a+\delta)\subset K$ for some $\delta>0$. Because $f$ is increasing,
$$\sup_V f\ge \sup_{(a-\delta,a+\delta)}f=f\big((a+\delta)^-\big),\qquad \inf_V f\le \inf_{(a-\delta,a+\delta)}f=f\big((a-\delta)^+\big),$$
where $f((a+\delta)^-)=\lim_{x\to(a+\delta)^-}f(x)$ and $f((a-\delta)^+)=\lim_{x\to(a-\delta)^+}f(x)$. Hence
$$\sup_{x,y\in V}|f(x)-f(y)|=\sup_V f-\inf_V f\ge f\big((a+\delta)^-\big)-f\big((a-\delta)^+\big).$$
As $\delta\to 0^+$, $(a+\delta)^-\to a^+$ and $(a-\delta)^+\to a^-$, so the right-hand side tends to $L^+-L^-$. Taking the infimum over all $V\in\mathcal N_a$,
$$\omega_f(a)\ge L^+-L^-.$$

**Upper bound.** For each $\delta>0$ with $(a-\delta,a+\delta)\subset K$, take $V_\delta:=(a-\delta,a+\delta)\in\mathcal N_a$. Then
$$\sup_{x,y\in V_\delta}|f(x)-f(y)|=f\big((a+\delta)^-\big)-f\big((a-\delta)^+\big).$$
Letting $\delta\to 0^+$, the right-hand side $\to L^+-L^-$. Therefore
$$\omega_f(a)=\inf_{V\in\mathcal N_a}\sup_{x,y\in V}|f(x)-f(y)|\le \inf_{\delta>0}\big[f\big((a+\delta)^-\big)-f\big((a-\delta)^+\big)\big]=L^+-L^-.$$

**Conclusion.** Combining both bounds,
$$\omega_f(a)=L^+-L^-=\Big|\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)-\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)\Big|.$$

For a **decreasing** $f$, the same argument gives $\omega_f(a)=L^- - L^+$, which equals $|L^+-L^-|$ since now $L^-\ge L^+$. Equivalently, apply the increasing case to $-f$. $\quad\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
